---
/**
 * Privacy policy. Says only what the code does: no accounts, no cookies, no
 * stored requests. The analytics line follows the build's env, so the page
 * never claims a beacon the site doesn't have. Every request is a public
 * GitHub issue: there is no email.
 */
import TextPage from "../layouts/TextPage.astro";
import { analyticsBeacon } from "../lib/analytics";
import { REPO_URL, newIssueUrl } from "../lib/site";

const GITHUB_PRIVACY = "https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement";
const PRIVACY_ISSUE = newIssueUrl("Privacy request: ");
const analyticsOn = analyticsBeacon(import.meta.env.PUBLIC_CF_ANALYTICS_TOKEN) !== null;
---

<TextPage
  title="Privacy"
  description="What freestack collects, who handles it, how long it stays, and how to ask about it."
  lede="No accounts and no cookies. freestack doesn't store anything about you. Two services see your visit, and they're listed below."
  updated="2026-11-18"
>
  <h2>Who runs freestack</h2>
  <p>
    freestack is a free, open-source project, not a business. It's run by the maintainers of the{" "}
    <a href={REPO_URL}>freestack repository</a> on GitHub. They decide what the site collects, which is almost nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No sign-up, no sign-in, no profiles. There's nothing to delete because nothing is kept.</li>
    <li>No cookies. Nothing tracks you across sites.</li>
    <li>
      The API, the MCP server and the CLI answer each request and forget it. The "student", "selling" and "Philippines"
      answers you send to the claim plan build the reply and are not saved.
    </li>
    <li>
      Fonts and search come from this site. Search runs in your browser. Compare picks live in the page address. When
      you leave an aisle with picks in the compare tray, your browser keeps their ids for that tab (session storage).
      They never reach us.
    </li>
    <li data-analytics={analyticsOn ? "on" : "off"}>
      {analyticsOn ? (
        <>
          Page views are counted with Cloudflare Web Analytics. It sets no cookies and doesn't fingerprint your device.
          We see totals per page, country, browser and referrer, never who you are.
        </>
      ) : (
        <>No analytics. We don't count visits.</>
      )}
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who handles your data</h2>
  <h3>Cloudflare</h3>
  <p>
    The site runs on Cloudflare Workers. Cloudflare sees each request (IP address, browser, page) to serve it and to
    block abuse. We haven't turned on Workers logs, so we keep no request logs. See{" "}
    <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>GitHub</h3>
  <p>
    Check a label and Add a tool send you to a GitHub issue form. You sign in to GitHub, not to us. What you post is
    public, like any GitHub issue. When every line of a check is "Still true", the label records your GitHub username
    and the date in the public data. See <a href={GITHUB_PRIVACY}>GitHub's privacy statement</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why we handle it</h2>
  <p>
    To serve the site, keep it working and block abuse. Where a law asks for a legal reason, ours is legitimate
    interest: running a free public site. Your username on a check is there because you chose to send it.
  </p>

  <h2>Data sent abroad</h2>
  <p>
    Cloudflare and GitHub are US companies with servers around the world. Your requests may be handled outside your
    country, under the safeguards in their own policies.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Requests to the site, API and MCP: not kept by us.</li>
    <li>
      Issues, pull requests and check records: public on GitHub until they're removed. The data's history lives in the{" "}
      <a href={REPO_URL}>public repository</a>, so a removed username can still show in older commits.
    </li>
    <li>Cloudflare and GitHub keep what they see under their own policies.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask what we hold about you. Usually that's nothing, or your GitHub username on a label.</li>
    <li>Ask us to fix it, remove it, or stop using it.</li>
    <li>You can delete your own issue comments on GitHub yourself.</li>
    <li>If you think we got it wrong, complain to the data protection authority where you live.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <a href={PRIVACY_ISSUE}>Open a privacy request</a> on GitHub. It's public, like everything here, so you can see how
    it's handled. Your GitHub username is enough: don't post anything private. We reply within one month.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>freestack isn't meant for children under 13. GitHub, which takes every request, needs you to be 13 too.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    When this page changes, the date at the top changes too. Every edit is a public commit in the{" "}
    <a href={REPO_URL}>source on GitHub</a>. Questions? See <a href="/contact">Contact</a>.
  </p>
</TextPage>
